  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nTemperatures and dewpoints have been a bit lower than guidance \ntoday. Have nudged these down a bit. Despite this, it will still be \nvery warm and humid with heat index values up to around 100 degrees \nduring the afternoon.\n\nSatellite data this morning has revealed a few convergent bands in\nthe low-level cu fields... likely left over from clusters of storms\nlast night. These are mostly located near or just north of \nInterstate 70. Mesoanalysis for the next few hours reveals peak mixed\nlayer CAPE of around 3,000 J/kg setting up between I-70 and I-71 \nlater this afternoon and evening. While an isolated storm is possible\npretty much anywhere during this time, perhaps the focus of\nconvective development will be near I-70 where the largest\ninstability gradient resides. DCAPE will once again approach or\nexceed 1,000 J/kg, so the primary threats will be brief heavy rain \nand gusty winds and the cores collapse.\n\nMoving into the evening hours, convection will develop toward the \nTri-State Region. Again, it will be isolated in coverage everywhere,\nbut the focus will shift south.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \nSunday into Monday, a weak mid-level disturbance will slowly shift \neast through our area with showers and storms possible each day. \nInstability will be much less as winds shift to the north behind a \nweak frontal boundary. This will limit the severe storm threat.",
